1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.468
In this lesson, you'll go through

2
00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:05.572
one of the most important steps
generating data and fine-tuning.

3
00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.842
Without much ado, let's get building.

4
00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:13.313
So the first thing is,
you usually have more data than you think.

5
00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:17.050
And it's actually important to first
take stock of the data you do have.

6
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.787
Figure out
if you have all the facts that are needed

7
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.090
to teach the model
about this new task of yours.

8
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.794
Whether if you gave that data to a person,
if they had, infinite

9
00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.062
time to go through all of it,

10
00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:31.264
could they actually figure out
what the right answer is?

11
00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:33.967
And you usually do have enough data.

12
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:37.137
The problem with the data, though, is
usually it's not in the right format.

13
00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:39.339
And historically, to put it in the right

14
00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:42.909
format was a very laborious
manual process.

15
00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:46.913
But today, you actually don't
have to do much manual data

16
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.681
labeling or clean up to reformat it.

17
00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:52.118
You have LLMs.
They are here to the rescue

18
00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:55.555
as long as you're able to specify
what the right format does look like.

19
00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.391
So this diagram down here,
you have tons of data,

20
00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.226
maybe only a little bit of clean data.

21
00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:05.226
But you can actually do a batch job with,
with an LLM or a pipeline of columns here

22
00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:10.103
to generate data to get that all to be
clean and ready to be put into the model.

23
00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:14.107
Identifying
the right format is kind of important.

24
00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.676
So let's go over a few different examples.

25
00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.778
It is highly application-specific.

26
00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:23.778
So for a SQL application for example,
you might expect a prompt

27
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.986
like given the schema in question,
write SQL query to answer

28
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.722
who is the highest paid player in the NBA?

29
00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:33.026
And the expected
response is a SQL statement.

30
00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:36.029
So this is what we've been exploring
in this course.

31
00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:40.433
But an alternative is just asking
who is the highest paid NBA player

32
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:44.437
and expecting the model
or the agent or pipeline of models,

33
00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.773
to be able to respond in natural language

34
00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.642
with, you know, for example,
the response of that SQL query.

35
00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:53.146
So that would be a different expected
response for a similar task.

36
00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:58.351
Another one for a different task
is this is a medical-related application.

37
00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:01.488
So you might, give symptoms of something

38
00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:04.591
and have to produce the ICD code for it.

39
00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:08.061
So that's another example of expected
prompt and expected response.

40
00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:11.331
This can be very, very application
specific and highly varied.

41
00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:12.866
But being able to understand

42
00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:16.035
what kind of prompts
you expect the model to be taking in

43
00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:19.906
and what kind of responses
you expect it to output

44
00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.909
is very important
in building out your applications.

45
00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:27.180
Okay, so specifically for SQL, here
and we'll also talk broadly,

46
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.748
when data seems kind of sparse.

47
00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:30.083
Again take stock of what you have

48
00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:34.387
and then get kind of creative, and work
backwards, to generate, generate data.

49
00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:39.192
So in text-to-SQL, you actually only need
these schema to make this happen.

50
00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:42.328
And I think this is really surprising
to people when they do hear about this.

51
00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:43.329
And here's why.

52
00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:47.467
So the LLM already knows how
to write SQL generally.

53
00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:47.734
Right.

54
00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:50.837
So it might not know about your schema,
the complexities

55
00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:51.971
of your schema in particular.

56
00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:54.073
But it knows how to write general SQL.

57
00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.910
And it can operate on a very basic schema.

58
00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.978
So basic pieces of that schema.

59
00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:03.650
And next is the schema actually contains
all the facts you need the LLM to know.

60
00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:04.050
Right.

61
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.219
Like this
screenshot down here of that schema

62
00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:10.323
that you put into the prompt actually
has all the facts you need it to know.

63
00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.425
Just like
if you were to hand this to a person,

64
00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:16.062
like a data analyst, to be able to operate
on. You're not expecting the

65
00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:19.332
LLM to learn facts from thin air or
just happen to know facts from thin air.

66
00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:22.135
That would be a much,
much more daunting task.

67
00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:25.171
But you actually do know
where all the facts are about your schema,

68
00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.373
and about your database scenario scheme.

69
00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.475
Okay.
So what does working backwards look like?

70
00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:32.645
So you can go from the schema
to generating new queries.

71
00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:34.814
And in this workflow,
in this prompt template,

72
00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:38.518
you'll see, you know, first telling the LLM
you're an analyst, again

73
00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:41.621
putting in the schema information,
giving an example.

74
00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:46.059
So giving an example of what a question
looks like and an example query looks like

75
00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:50.196
and then asking it,
how about you write a query that's similar

76
00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:52.198
but different to those above.

77
00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:55.034
And format the response
in this Json object.

78
00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.169
So write me some queries.

79
00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:02.108
Okay, so now that you generated
your SQL queries,

80
00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:05.578
you can actually take those
to generate user questions in turn.

81
00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:08.648
So let's take a look
at what the prompt for that looks like.

82
00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:11.517
You can again
tell the LLM you're an NBA analyst.

83
00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.353
You give it the schema.

84
00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:15.321
You're probably an expert at this already.

85
00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:18.024
You give it
an example of a question and a query,

86
00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:22.729
and then you put your generated, query,
SQL query in and then ask for a question

87
00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:26.766
that this query could be used to answer,
and it would output a question like,

88
00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:30.336
how many players on the Chicago
Bulls are 25 or younger?

89
00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:31.738
All right.

90
00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:35.675
So what are some practical tips
that you can follow?

91
00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:37.377
One is an example

92
00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:38.878
you just saw adding, you know,

93
00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:42.682
query user
question examples can be really helpful.

94
00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:45.752
This is essentially called few-shot
or in-context learning.

95
00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:49.722
So adding those examples is helpful
for the LLM to understand

96
00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:51.357
what right looks like.

97
00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:56.357
And one pro tip here is actually including
corrected hallucinatory examples.

98
00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:58.064
So hallucinations

99
00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:02.001
that you had previously seen
but then correcting them helps the LLM

100
00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:06.072
actually understand what it needs to learn
and to create examples that are similar

101
00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:10.877
to what's been hallucinated on. Generating
variations is incredibly important.

102
00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:12.211
You want to be able to reach

103
00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:15.448
as much breath as possible
without needing to write those yourself.

104
00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:20.086
So getting the LLM to assume
different persona, for example,

105
00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:23.890
not just NBA analyst
to be able to generate different questions

106
00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:26.125
and different
queries, can be really helpful as well.

107
00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:27.927
And it depends on who your end user
is, right?

108
00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:31.331
Maybe it's not just NBA analyst,
maybe it's, you know, some people are

109
00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:36.331
more senior and want an executive
kind of understanding of what's going on

110
00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:37.537
in this database.

111
00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:40.707
So, this is how you can also generate
additional variations.

112
00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:42.675
Next is around filtering generation.

113
00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.745
So as you generate more and more data,

114
00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:49.148
inevitably these models the whole point
as you're trying to improve them.

115
00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:52.752
So inevitably you're going to get
some things that are, incorrect.

116
00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:57.752
And so, being able to filter and rely
on your filters, automatic filters, just

117
00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:01.027
scalably downsample your generated

118
00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:05.598
examples into a higher quality
data set is incredibly important.

119
00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:08.201
and what's special here
is that you can actually use very similar

120
00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.770
methods that you had implemented
during evaluation,

121
00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:15.208
both those that are LLM based and also
those that are more deterministic.

122
00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:17.477
So you can check
if something's valid SQL or not.

123
00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:18.478
For example.

124
00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:22.515
And finally, it's worth really digging
through what worked and what didn't,

125
00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:26.652
and kind of using your head to classify
what's working, what what isn't

126
00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:30.123
and what are patterns of what's working,
what are patterns of what isn't.

127
00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.526
This is called error analysis
in AI and machine learning

128
00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:37.196
and it's an important skill to help you,
figure out what to generate next,

129
00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:38.331
what to filter next.

130
00:06:38.331 --> 00:06:42.335
And usually, you know, in this specific
case, the more complex queries are harder.

131
00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:43.770
So that's a good observation.

132
00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.438
And this helps you adjust the prompts

133
00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:48.941
and actually go more specific in
trying to generate those, those queries.

134
00:06:49.776 --> 00:06:53.446
So now that you have all the data,
what does the fine-tuning process

135
00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:54.480
actually look like.

136
00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:55.948
Here are some minimum requirements.

137
00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:59.452
Actually you you only need one data point
to do memory tuning.

138
00:06:59.452 --> 00:07:02.155
You also only need one data point
to technically instruction fine-tune.

139
00:07:02.155 --> 00:07:06.259
It will just, not make the model, capable
of continuing to generalize very well

140
00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.026
after that.

141
00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:07.927
So instruction, fine-tuning

142
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.930
usually I do suggest around
a thousand data points.

143
00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.734
Fine-tuning really needs, you know,
these pairs of prompts and responses

144
00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.002
so essentially that format

145
00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:19.872
you were specifying before, memory
tuning in particular, needs these prompts

146
00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:23.910
and responses to include the facts
that you want the LLM to actually learn,

147
00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:25.678
especially in the responses.

148
00:07:25.678 --> 00:07:28.281
And then what does fine-tuning,
like ease of use look like?

149
00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:31.517
Well, you could use a library
like Lamini, which manages, fine

150
00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:32.185
tuning for you.

151
00:07:32.185 --> 00:07:34.787
So it's just a one-line
API or Python call.

152
00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.189
And you can also roll your own,

153
00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:39.192
and hyperparameter tuning,
calling the model forwards and backwards.

154
00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:43.863
And here's an example of running fine
tuning so you can instantiate the model.

155
00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:47.667
You get the dataset, and then you'd call
LLM dot train on that dataset.

156
00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:49.669
And your arguments.

157
00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:50.036
Okay.

158
00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:52.672
So fine-tuning doesn't come
totally for free.

159
00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:56.843
So understanding, you know,
what are the time and compute requirements

160
00:07:56.843 --> 00:07:59.979
you need to do fine
tuning, instruction fine-tuning,

161
00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:03.516
specifically with LoRA,
gets you to an accuracy of about 50%.

162
00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.519
But it is really fast,
or it only takes a couple minutes.

163
00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:07.386
2.5 minutes.

164
00:08:07.386 --> 00:08:10.790
and it takes 19.2 petaflops of compute.

165
00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:12.592
And this is all benchmarking

166
00:08:12.592 --> 00:08:17.592
on one Nvidia A100 with,
with high performance of about 40% MFU

167
00:08:17.964 --> 00:08:22.268
or the equivalent
AMD GPU is in mid to 50. For memory tuning,

168
00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:27.268
it's actually quite more intensive
when you use the unoptimized version.

169
00:08:27.406 --> 00:08:32.406
and this is still, actually using LoRA,
but just unoptimized and the accuracy

170
00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:36.449
on a thousand facts can go up much higher
because a whole point

171
00:08:36.449 --> 00:08:38.150
is to reduce hallucination.

172
00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:42.388
But the reason why it takes so much longer
and so much more compute

173
00:08:42.388 --> 00:08:45.691
you can see 1.9 to exa
flops are in that second row

174
00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:49.996
and four hours is because you're bringing
the loss to zero on the specific facts.

175
00:08:50.162 --> 00:08:52.331
With Lamini memory tuning,
we've implemented,

176
00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:55.434
you know, those different, optimizations
that you saw previously.

177
00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:59.105
And the time needed
is only a minute and 24 petaflops.

178
00:09:00.306 --> 00:09:00.673
Okay.

179
00:09:00.673 --> 00:09:01.674
What should you expect?

180
00:09:01.674 --> 00:09:03.876
So that all feels fine and good,

181
00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:06.579
hugs and rainbows.
but it's not just. Boom.

182
00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:09.415
One time you fine tune it
and your life is good.

183
00:09:09.415 --> 00:09:12.051
It realistically takes many iterations.

184
00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:14.153
And that's why iteration, it's art.

185
00:09:14.153 --> 00:09:16.289
Like, that concept is so, so important.

186
00:09:16.289 --> 00:09:18.758
So it can take,
you know, 10 to 30 iterations

187
00:09:18.758 --> 00:09:22.662
I think is about 20 for what you're about
to do over different data pipelines

188
00:09:22.662 --> 00:09:27.333
to get to that 90, 95% accuracy.
Fine-tuning in parallel,

189
00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:30.002
so fine-tuning
multiple variants in parallel can speed up

190
00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:31.037
your experimentation.

191
00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:35.107
You can actually do iterations in parallel
in a way they don't have to necessarily

192
00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:35.808
be sequential.

193
00:09:35.808 --> 00:09:39.478
If you're experimenting with, hey,
if I change my data pipeline in this way,

194
00:09:39.679 --> 00:09:43.282
will it influence the model
to improve in this way versus that way?

195
00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:44.984
And those are orthogonal paths.

196
00:09:44.984 --> 00:09:48.054
You can actually experiment
with, during fine-tuning.

197
00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:50.890
And the exciting part is
you can start talking about nines.

198
00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:55.890
So you can, actually start
eking out accuracy from 95 to to 99.9

199
00:09:56.362 --> 00:10:00.032
and this makes it very exciting
because there are many applications,

200
00:10:00.032 --> 00:10:03.869
new applications that get unlocked,
with that new frontier of accuracy.

201
00:10:04.003 --> 00:10:08.641
Note that with every nine that you do
add on, it is far more effort

202
00:10:08.641 --> 00:10:09.609
than the previous one.

203
00:10:09.609 --> 00:10:12.378
So it is it is a
it is a game of effort here

204
00:10:12.378 --> 00:10:13.846
to get to that level of accuracy.

205
00:10:13.846 --> 00:10:17.683
But that usually goes hand in hand
with how critical it is to be accurate

206
00:10:17.683 --> 00:10:19.285
for that use case.

207
00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:22.722
Now, how can you become a wizard at this?

208
00:10:23.022 --> 00:10:27.059
Well, if you have the facts
somewhere, you have enough data.

209
00:10:27.326 --> 00:10:31.197
You should be able to find a way
to use a LLMs to take care of the rest.

210
00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:35.201
and it does take some creativity
sometimes to think of, oh, how can I use

211
00:10:35.201 --> 00:10:38.237
an LLM in these ways
to, you know, pipe them together

212
00:10:38.237 --> 00:10:41.340
to get the data that I do want,
from the data I already have.

213
00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:44.477
and from that, your data pipelines
can get pretty intricate.

214
00:10:44.477 --> 00:10:47.146
And you need you can be calling
the model many, many times.

215
00:10:47.146 --> 00:10:51.017
But because it's a one-time batch process
to transform your data, it can,

216
00:10:51.017 --> 00:10:52.184
actually be worth it.

217
00:10:52.184 --> 00:10:54.020
What might make you feel like
it's more worth

218
00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:57.490
it is if you're running a startup or,
an enterprise.

219
00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:58.691
this is your AI moat.

220
00:10:58.691 --> 00:11:01.327
This can Help you actually build up
something quite unique.

221
00:11:01.327 --> 00:11:05.765
And another skill to be a wizard
is just spotting these LLM mistakes.

222
00:11:05.765 --> 00:11:07.133
Doing that error analysis

223
00:11:07.133 --> 00:11:10.836
and hallucinations to teach your LLM
what correct actually looks like.

224
00:11:10.936 --> 00:11:13.239
If you don't know what
that frontier looks like,

225
00:11:13.239 --> 00:11:16.475
it's very hard to tell the LLM
what the right objective is.

226
00:11:16.509 --> 00:11:19.412
Again,
the LLM is super, super good at optimizing,

227
00:11:19.412 --> 00:11:21.113
But it doesn't know what to optimize for.

228
00:11:21.113 --> 00:11:24.984
And if you can tell it what to optimize
for, that is within reach for it,

229
00:11:25.184 --> 00:11:26.318
then I can go there.

230
00:11:26.318 --> 00:11:30.956
One you know, tip that's not necessarily
put here is for certain applications.

231
00:11:31.023 --> 00:11:34.126
You are sitting next to, the person who is

232
00:11:34.126 --> 00:11:37.630
evaluating the model who can tell you
